## Claim
Text: "Jammu and Kashmir (J&K) in Pakistan/India was removed from the United Nations (UN) list of “unresolved disputes”."
Claim author: Paresh Rawal ‏
Claim date: October 29, 2020
Claim origin: https://archive.ph/3oBfZ

## Initial Q&A
### Was Jammu and Kashmir listed on any official United Nations list of unresolved disputes or matters before the United Nations Security Council prior to October 2020?
Answer: Yes, Jammu and Kashmir was previously included on the United Nations list of unresolved disputes under the observation of the Security Council. According to the source, its omission from this list prompted a protest from Pakistan's acting envoy to the U.N., who stated that "Jammu and Kashmir is one of the oldest disputes on the agenda of the Security Council."

Source URL: https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/Jammu-and-Kashmir-out-of-U.N.-list-of-disputes/article15687886.ece

### Did the United Nations officially remove Jammu and Kashmir from any list of unresolved disputes or active agendas on or around October 2020?
Answer: Based on the provided search result, the United Nations did not remove Jammu and Kashmir from its active agenda around October 2020. In fact, official communications regarding the dispute continued to be issued "as official documents of the Security Council under the agenda item “India-Pakistan Question”" through at least November 20, 2020.

Source URL: https://www.pakungeneva.pk/ContentPage.aspx?ID=39

### What is the official position of the United Nations regarding the political and territorial status of Jammu and Kashmir as of October 2020?
Answer: Based on the provided search result, the United Nations continues to recognize Jammu and Kashmir as a disputed territory, maintaining the issue on the UN Security Council's agenda under the item "India-Pakistan Question." Since 1948, the Security Council has adopted over a dozen resolutions on the "Jammu & Kashmir dispute," which guarantee "the Kashmiri people’s inalienable right to self-determination."

Source URL: https://www.pakungeneva.pk/ContentPage.aspx?ID=39

### Are there any historical instances prior to 2020 where Jammu and Kashmir was temporarily or permanently omitted from United Nations annual reports or agendas, and what were the reasons for those omissions?
Answer: Yes, there is a documented historical instance prior to 2020 where Jammu and Kashmir was omitted from a United Nations list of unresolved disputes under the observation of the Security Council. According to Pakistan's acting envoy to the U.N., Amjad Hussain B Sial, who raised a protest over the matter, the exclusion of the dispute from the context of unresolved long-running situations was understood to be an "inadvertent omission."

Source URL: https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/Jammu-and-Kashmir-out-of-U.N.-list-of-disputes/article15687886.ece

### How does the United Nations Military Observer Group in India and Pakistan (UNMOGIP) currently classify the territory and conflict of Jammu and Kashmir?
Answer: According to the provided source, the region of Kashmir is characterized as "disputed by the two countries" of India and Pakistan, and UNMOGIP has "supervised the State of Jammu and Kashmir ever since 1949." Because the mission's mandate is "open-ended and does not require continuous renewal," UNMOGIP continues to "observe the border and report any ceasefire violations" along the Line of Control.

Source URL: https://betterworldcampaign.org/mission/kashmir-unmogip

## Final Judgement
### Step 1: Summary of Key Insights
The retrieved evidence shows that the United Nations continues to officially recognize Jammu and Kashmir as a disputed territory, maintaining the issue on the UN Security Council's active agenda under the item "India-Pakistan Question." Official UN communications regarding the dispute continued to be issued under this agenda item through at least November 2020. While there was a historical instance prior to 2020 where Jammu and Kashmir was omitted from a UN list of unresolved disputes, this was officially protested by Pakistan and understood to be an "inadvertent omission" rather than a formal or permanent removal. Furthermore, the United Nations Military Observer Group in India and Pakistan (UNMOGIP) continues its open-ended mandate to observe and report on the disputed territory. Because the evidence is clear and directly addresses the claim, no further investigation is required, and we can proceed to step 3.

### Step 3: Conclusion
Based on the recorded evidence, the claim that Jammu and Kashmir was removed from the United Nations list of "unresolved disputes" is explicitly contradicted by official UN operations and agendas. The UN continues to classify Jammu and Kashmir as a disputed territory and maintains the issue on the Security Council's active agenda. The historical omission of the dispute from a UN list prior to 2020 was merely an inadvertent error and not an official policy change or permanent removal. Therefore, the decision is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim is refuted because the United Nations continues to officially recognize Jammu and Kashmir as a disputed territory, maintaining the issue on the UN Security Council's active agenda under the item "India-Pakistan Question," as documented by the [Pakistan Mission to the UN Geneva](https://www.pakungeneva.pk/ContentPage.aspx?ID=39). Although Jammu and Kashmir was previously left off a UN list of unresolved disputes, [The Hindu](https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/Jammu-and-Kashmir-out-of-U.N.-list-of-disputes/article15687886.ece) reported that this exclusion was protested by Pakistan and clarified as an "inadvertent omission" rather than a permanent policy change. Additionally, the [Better World Campaign](https://betterworldcampaign.org/mission/kashmir-unmogip) notes that the United Nations Military Observer Group in India and Pakistan (UNMOGIP) continues its open-ended mandate to supervise the state and report ceasefire violations along the Line of Control.